<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Damjing</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">579–631</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Donchō</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Damjing was a Buddhist monk from Goguryeo who went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 610 together with another monk. According to the Nihon shoki, he was learned in the Confucian classics and introduced the making of paper, ink and pigments, as well as millstones. Tradition also credits him with paintings at Hōryū-ji, though this is not established.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/damjing.jpg" alt="Damjing" width="192" height="360" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown pre-modern painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cai_Lun_with_Donch%C5%8D_and_Mochizuki_Seibee_(Minobu_Museum_of_History_and_Folklore).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
